**Yes, a backend change is absolutely required.**
If you only change the frontend, the backend will still block the request and throw an error.
If you look at the Java code you shared earlier for updateBatchSchedule (and createBatchRequest), you have this exact line:
```java
workflowEngine.validateScheduledTime(parsedTime);

```
I can guarantee that inside that validateScheduledTime method in your Java code, there is logic checking if the time is at least 30 minutes in the future. If a Final Approver tries to schedule it for 5 minutes from now, the frontend will allow it, but **your Java backend will throw an exception.**
### How to fix the Backend (2 Simple Steps)
You need to update your Java workflowEngine so it knows *who* is scheduling the batch.
**Step 1: Update the validateScheduledTime method signature**
Add a boolean flag so the engine knows if it should apply the strict 30-minute rule or just a simple "not in the past" rule.
```java
// In your VoucherWorkflowEngine.java
public void validateScheduledTime(LocalDateTime scheduledTime, boolean isFinalApproval) {
    LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.now();
    
    // Rule for Final Approver: Just make sure it's not in the past
    if (isFinalApproval) {
        if (scheduledTime.isBefore(now)) {
            throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("Schedule time cannot be in the past.");
        }
    } 
    // Rule for Creators/Makers: Must be 30 mins in the future
    else {
        if (scheduledTime.isBefore(now.plusMinutes(30))) {
            throw new CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("Schedule time must be at least 30 minutes from now.");
        }
    }
    
    // (Keep your existing check for end of day here)
}

```
**Step 2: Pass the flag from your Controller/Service**
When the Final Approver calls the API to approve and schedule the batch, pass true.
```java
// Example in your Approver Service method:
workflowEngine.validateScheduledTime(parsedTime, true); 

```
When the Creator/Maker is saving their initial batch, pass false.
```java
// Example in your createBatchRequest method:
workflowEngine.validateScheduledTime(parsedTime, false); 

```
If you don't make this Java change, your Final Approvers will get a red error box saying *"Schedule time must be at least 30 minutes from now"* every time they try to execute a batch immediately!
